Meniere's disease
Opthalmology & ENT
Episodic vertigo (minutes to hours), Fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss, Roaring tinnitus/aural fullness

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-025
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, debilitating rotational vertigo with intractable vomiting and inability to stand.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an acute onset of severe rotational vertigo, profound nausea, and unilateral deafness. Urgent home assessment was mandatory to differentiate a peripheral vestibular crisis (Meniere's) from a central posterior circulation stroke (cerebellar or brainstem infarction), and to provide immediate parenteral antiemetic therapy to halt severe, dehydrating vomiting prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The whole room is spinning violently, I can't stop vomiting, and my right ear is roaring like a jet engine."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an episodic, acute attack of severe, debilitating rotational vertigo that commenced 2 hours ago. Accompanied by severe systemic nausea and unremitting non-bilious vomiting. Endorses a severe, loud roaring tinnitus and an overwhelming sensation of deep aural fullness directly in the right ear. Notes fluctuating, muffled hearing in the right ear that worsens during these attacks. History of similar episodes lasting 2-6 hours over the past year. Denies facial droop, slurred speech, limb weakness, or visual changes.
Functional Status: Bedbound, actively vomiting into a basin, unable to ambulate safely.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk; severe dehydration risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Severely distressed, lying perfectly still with eyes closed, holding a vomit bowl.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals horizontal nystagmus with the fast phase beating away from the affected right ear. Cranial nerves otherwise intact; no facial asymmetry. Cerebellar testing (finger-to-nose, heel-to-shin) is intact despite severe dizziness. Otoscopy reveals a pristine, normal right tympanic membrane, effectively ruling out middle ear pathology. Gross hearing is subjectively reduced on the right. No focal motor or sensory deficits in the limbs.
Environmental Observations: Darkened room, bucket by the bed, patient avoiding all head movement.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Has a script for Betahistine but has been non-compliant.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Meniere's Disease - Status: Acute vertiginous crisis
Differentials:

Posterior Circulation Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of central neurological signs (dysarthria, dysphagia, diplopia, long tract signs), intact cerebellar function, and the classic triad of peripheral auditory symptoms (tinnitus, fullness, deafness).

Vestibular Neuritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of auditory symptoms (roaring tinnitus and hearing loss), which are strictly absent in pure vestibular neuritis.

Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo (BPPV) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The continuous nature of the vertigo lasting hours (BPPV lasts <1 minute), the presence of profound auditory symptoms, and spontaneous nystagmus at rest.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Dehydration, fall risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented entirely incapacitated by a severe vertiginous crisis and intractable vomiting. The immediate clinical priority was to rule out a posterior circulation stroke, which was achieved through a careful bedside neurological and cerebellar examination. The presence of the pathognomonic triad (vertigo, roaring tinnitus, aural fullness) confirmed an acute endolymphatic hydrops crisis (Meniere's). Because the patient could not tolerate oral medications, immediate parenteral intervention was required to arrest the vomiting, prevent severe dehydration, and chemically suppress the vestibular system, safely managing the crisis in the home and avoiding an emergency department admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular Prochlorperazine 12.5mg STAT to provide rapid vestibular suppression and antiemesis. Prescribed a short course of oral Prochlorperazine 5mg TDS for 3 days to manage residual dizziness. Advised strict adherence to previously prescribed Betahistine 16mg TDS once vomiting ceases.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the chronic endolymphatic pressure, the patient must completely remove the salt shaker from their dining table and visually inspect the sodium content on every food label before eating. The immediate goal of preventing another terrifying, debilitating vertiginous attack serves as powerful negative reinforcement, ensuring strict adherence to the low-sodium diet required to stabilize the inner ear fluid.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week for formal audiometry and review of long-term prophylactic management.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops slurred speech, double vision, sudden weakness in an arm or leg, or if the severe vomiting continues for another 6 hours despite the injection.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; formal audiometry is required to document the exact degree of hearing loss.
Clinical Course: The acute spinning and vomiting will subside within a few hours following the injection. You will likely feel exhausted and mildly off-balance for a few days.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you suddenly experience difficulty speaking, see double, or lose strength in your limbs, this is not Meniere's; it is a stroke, and you must call 000 immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The fluid compartments in your inner ear have become over-pressurized, causing the ear to send chaotic balance and sound signals to your brain. The injection will sedate the balance nerve to stop the spinning and vomiting.
Actionable Steps: Lie perfectly still in a dark room until the injection works. When the vomiting stops, you must adopt a strict low-salt diet to prevent fluid from building up in your ear again. Remove the salt shaker from your table today.

SMS INFORMATION

